{"parentUuid":"6db32de4-124f-406c-bfbd-3d03aa560514","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"skill_listing","content":"- anime-hardsub-hevc: Depuis un .mkv d'anime fansub (VOSTFR, sous-titres ASS/SSA embarqués), produit une copie qui garde uniquement l'audio japonais, incruste (\"burn\") les sous-titres FR en dur avec une police Anthropic et des pills (fonds arrondis façon Zelda TOTK), et encode en HEVC via le hardware Mac (VideoToolbox / Media Engine). Utiliser quand Matthieu demande de renommer/graver/remuxer/encoder un épisode d'anime avec sous-titres FR incrustés et piste audio JP seule.\n- archive-organize: Ranger, renommer ou déplacer une archive ZenRay DÉJÀ uploadée (manifestes .file.json dans zenray-data) SANS rien re-uploader, en miroir d'une arborescence locale. Aussi : créer une nouvelle archive (archive-folder) avec la convention DJI (date obligatoire, rangé sous Build/DJI/<date>, segments Photos/Videos/BestOf = miroir du dossier source). Utiliser quand Matthieu dit « range / réorganise / renomme / déplace cette archive », « mets l'archive sous tel dossier », « trie l'archive comme sur mon Mac », ou archive un dossier DJI.\n- interview-audio: Pipeline audio complet pour les musiques de fond et la voix d'une interview/vidéo. Télécharge une piste (yt-dlp, recherche ou URL) en WAV, applique fade in/out, et normalise — soit en normalisation CLASSIQUE (pic à un dBFS cible, ex -12), soit en LOUDNESS (loudnorm LUFS, ex -28 pour une BGM sous la voix, -16 pour la voix). Sait aussi extraire la piste audio d'un MP4 et lui appliquer une chaîne \"voix podcast\" (coupe-bas, chaleur grave, présence, compression douce, loudnorm -16). Utiliser dès que Matthieu veut préparer des musiques sous une interview, des SFX d'intro avec fades, normaliser des WAV à un niveau, ou traiter une voix en son podcast.\n- kanji-stroke-order-html: Generate a complete standalone HTML study sheet for a Japanese kanji — stroke-order SVG inlined directly into the HTML body, plus a structured template with ON reading, KUN reading, stroke count, radical decomposition, mnemonic, 3-4 examples, and a sentence using the kanji. Produces a single self-contained .html file that opens correctly in any browser AND in Claude Code's preview (no external file references). Use this skill whenever Matthieu learns a new kanji during his Japanese study sessions and needs the rich study sheet saved into Memory/Japanese/KanjiProgress/. For raw SVG without HTML wrapper, use the sibling skill `kanji-stroke-order-svg` instead.\n- kanji-stroke-order-svg: Generate a standalone textbook-style stroke-order SVG for any Japanese character (kanji, hiragana, or katakana). Produces one panel per stroke — completed strokes in black, current stroke highlighted with red number and directional arrow, remaining strokes ghosted in light gray. Use this skill when the user wants ONLY the stroke-order diagram as a raw SVG (no HTML wrapper, no metadata). For the full HTML kanji study sheet with ON/KUN/mnemonic/examples bundled in, use the sibling skill `kanji-stroke-order-html` instead.\n- masterclass-builder: Use this skill to design rigorous, deep masterclasses and multi-module curricula (roughly 90 minutes per module, extensible to 15-20h) that build durable understanding and autonomous capability, not passive \"AI slop.\" Trigger this whenever the user wants to create a masterclass, a course, a deep curriculum, a structured learning program, progressive exercises on a topic, a study plan, or wants to genuinely LEARN a domain (not just get a one-shot answer), even if they do not use the word \"course.\" Covers learner profiling, 80/20 syllabus design, mechanism-level explanation (why before how), scaffolded progressive exercises, active recall, decision-forcing outputs, multi-agent adversarially verified research, fact verification, and an anti-slop self-audit.\n- srt-yt-anime: Extrait le sous-titre d'un ou plusieurs fichiers .mkv (anime fansub, ASS/SSA le plus souvent) et produit un .srt en texte brut, sans balises <font>/<b>, prêt à uploader dans YouTube Studio. YouTube Studio n'interprète pas les balises de style ASS/SRT et les affiche telles quelles à l'écran si on ne les retire pas. Utiliser dès que Matthieu veut des sous-titres SRT propres pour YouTube depuis un mkv, un épisode, ou une saison entière.\n- think-of-everything: Run a systematic \"think of everything\" impact and premortem pass before or after implementing a code change, feature, or architecture decision. Reads the request four ways (literal, inverse, adjacent, reframed), traces every real interdependency in the actual codebase by grepping or searching it directly instead of guessing from memory, then checks the change against a fixed impact-surface list covering data and state, concurrency, quotas, security, error handling, backward compatibility, performance, cost, observability, UX edge cases, tests, and rollback. Use this whenever the user says \"pense à tout\", \"think of everything\", \"qu'est-ce que j'ai oublié\", \"what am I missing\", asks what a change might break, or wants a premortem, dependency check, or impact analysis. Also trigger proactively, even without the exact phrase, right before shipping anything that touches shared state, storage or quotas, concurrency, auth or permissions, payments, or a public API/route.\n- ytb-dlp: <!-- MANPAGE: BEGIN EXCLUDED SECTION -->\n- zenray-backup-mirror: Déclencher, monitorer ou arrêter le second système de backup ZenRayProgress (Scripts/backup-mirror.mjs — miroir froid indépendant, second exemplaire des binaires ZenRayShards sur des repos privés séparés). Déclencheurs — \"lance le backup\", \"vérifie le second backup\", \"sauvegarde en double tel dossier\", \"où en est le backup-mirror\", \"monitore le backup\". JAMAIS lancer le run complet sans confirmation explicite de Matthieu — toujours proposer --dry-run --limit=N en premier.\n- zenrayshards-upload-media: Skill UNIQUE d'upload média ZenRayProgress — détecte automatiquement l'entrée et route vers le bon pipeline. FICHIERS LOCAUX (vidéo/image, mélangés OK) -> ZenRayShards v2 (repo GitHub NEUF par lot zenrayshards-<type>-<hex12>, zéro clone/check, vid=privé+chiffré+hvc1, img=public jsDelivr). LIEN YouTube -> nœud .media.json type:'youtube' (vidéo) ou audioOnly:true (musique/piste/chanson). Déclencheurs — \"upload zenrayshards ...\", \"upload vid zenrayshards '<dossier>' '<fichier>' ...\", \"uploade ce(s) fichier(s)/cette vidéo/cette musique\", tout chemin local ou lien youtube.com/youtu.be avec intention d'ajout au Create/Drive. Si le dossier Create cible manque, DEMANDER via AskUserQuestion, jamais deviner. Remplace et absorbe les anciens skills zenray-upload-media, zenray-upload-video, zenray-upload-audio (supprimés le 2026-07-06).\n- translate: Tu es Traducteur FR/EN/JP. Comme un outil en CLI concis.\n- deep-research: Deep research harness — fan-out web searches, fetch sources, adversarially verify claims, synthesize a cited report. - When the user wants a deep, multi-source, fact-checked research report on any topic. BEFORE invoking, check if the question is specific enough to research directly — if underspecified (e.g., \"what car to buy\" without budget/use-case/region), ask 2-3 clarifying questions to narrow scope. Then pass the refined question as args, weaving the answers in.\n- anthropic-skills:consolidate-memory: Reflective pass over your memory files — merge duplicates, fix stale facts, prune the index.\n- anthropic-skills:docx: Use this skill whenever the user wants to create, read, edit, or manipulate Word documents (.docx files) or Word templates (.dotx files). Triggers include: any mention of 'Word doc', 'word document', '.docx', '.dotx', or requests to produce professional documents with formatting like tables of contents, headings, page numbers, or letterheads. Also use when extracting or reorganizing content from .docx or .dotx files, inserting or replacing images in documents, performing find-and-replace in Word files, working with tracked changes or comments, or converting content into a polished Word document. If the user asks for a 'report', 'memo', 'letter', 'template', or similar deliverable as a Word or .docx file, use this skill. Do NOT use for PDFs, spreadsheets, Google Docs, or general coding tasks unrelated to document generation.\n- anthropic-skills:kanji-stroke-order: Generate a textbook-style stroke-order diagram (SVG) for any Japanese character (kanji, hiragana, or katakana). Produces one panel per stroke showing completed strokes in black, the current stroke highlighted with a red number and directional arrow at its starting point, and remaining strokes ghosted in light gray. Use this skill whenever the user asks how to write a Japanese character, requests stroke order, asks \"in what order do I draw X\", wants to learn or memorize a kanji, asks to decompose a character into strokes, says \"show me the strokes for X\", mentions 書き順 / kakijun, or shows a stroke-order chart and asks for one for a different character. Trigger even when the user only provides a single character and the intent is implicit from the language-learning context.\n- anthropic-skills:mcp-builder: Guide for creating high-quality MCP (Model Context Protocol) servers that enable LLMs to interact with external services through well-designed tools. Use when building MCP servers to integrate external APIs or services, whether in Python (FastMCP) or Node/TypeScript (MCP SDK).\n- anthropic-skills:pdf: Use this skill whenever the user wants to do anything with PDF files. This includes reading or extracting text/tables from PDFs, combining or merging multiple PDFs into one, splitting PDFs apart, rotating pages, adding watermarks, creating new PDFs, filling PDF forms, encrypting/decrypting PDFs, extracting images, and OCR on scanned PDFs to make them searchable. If the user mentions a .pdf file or asks to produce one, use this skill.\n- anthropic-skills:pptx: Use this skill any time a .pptx or .potx file is involved in any way — as input, output, or both. This includes: creating slide decks, pitch decks, or presentations; reading, parsing, or extracting text from any .pptx or .potx file (even if the extracted content will be used elsewhere, like in an email or summary); editing, modifying, or updating existing presentations; combining or splitting slide files; working with templates (.potx), layouts, speaker notes, or comments. Trigger whenever the user mentions \"deck,\" \"slides,\" \"presentation,\" or references a .pptx or .potx filename, regardless of what they plan to do with the content afterward. If a .pptx or .potx file needs to be opened, created, or touched, use this skill.\n- anthropic-skills:schedule: Create or update a scheduled task that runs automatically. Use when the user says things like \"every day\", \"each morning\", \"remind me in an hour\", \"run this at noon\", or wants to reschedule an existing task.\n- anthropic-skills:setup-cowork: Guided Cowork setup — install role-matched plugins, connect your tools, try a skill.\n- anthropic-skills:skill-creator: Create new skills, modify and improve existing skills, and measure skill performance. Use when users want to create a skill from scratch, edit, or optimize an existing skill, run evals to test a skill, benchmark skill performance with variance analysis, or optimize a skill's description for better triggering accuracy.\n- anthropic-skills:xlsx: Use this skill any time a spreadsheet file is the primary input or output. This means any task where the user wants to: open, read, edit, or fix an existing .xlsx, .xlsm, .xltx, .csv, or .tsv file (e.g., adding columns, computing formulas, formatting, charting, cleaning messy data); create a new spreadsheet from scratch or from other data sources; or convert between tabular file formats. Trigger especially when the user references a spreadsheet file by name or path — even casually (like \"the xlsx in my downloads\") — and wants something done to it or produced from it. Also trigger for cleaning or restructuring messy tabular data files (malformed rows, misplaced headers, junk data) into proper spreadsheets. The deliverable must be a spreadsheet file. Do NOT trigger when the primary deliverable is a Word document, HTML report, standalone Python script, database pipeline, or Google Sheets API integration, even if tabular data is involved.\n- dataviz: Use this skill whenever you are about to create ANY chart, graph, plot, dashboard, or data visualization, in ANY output medium — an HTML or React artifact, inline SVG, plotting code in any library (matplotlib, plotly, d3, Recharts, …), an image/PNG you will render and upload, or a chart shared into Slack. Read it BEFORE writing the first line of chart code, choosing chart colors, building a stat tile / meter / KPI row, or laying out a dashboard. Produces visualizations that read as one system — elegant, accessible, consistent in light and dark — using a brand-neutral placeholder palette you swap for your own. Teaches a design-system-agnostic method: a form heuristic, a color formula with a runnable validator, mark specs, and interaction rules. A validated default palette is documented in `references/palette.md` — swap that file's values for your brand's. Triggers on: \"chart\", \"graph\", \"plot\", \"data viz\", \"visualization\", \"dashboard\", \"analytics\", \"visualize data\", \"categorical colors\", \"sequential / diverging palette\", \"stat tile\", \"sparkline\", \"heatmap\", \"legend\", \"axis\", \"tooltip\", \"chart colors\", \"color by series\".\n- artifact-design: Design guidance and fundamentals for Artifacts.\n- update-config: Use this skill to configure the Claude Code harness via settings.json. Automated behaviors (\"from now on when X\", \"each time X\", \"whenever X\", \"before/after X\") require hooks configured in settings.json - the harness executes these, not Claude, so memory/preferences cannot fulfill them. Also use for: permissions (\"allow X\", \"add permission\", \"move permission to\"), env vars (\"set X=Y\"), hook troubleshooting, or any changes to settings.json/settings.local.json files. Examples: \"allow npm commands\", \"add bq permission to global settings\", \"move permission to user settings\", \"set DEBUG=true\", \"when claude stops show X\". For simple settings like theme/model, suggest the /config command.\n- keybindings-help: Use when the user wants to customize keyboard shortcuts, rebind keys, add chord bindings, or modify ~/.claude/keybindings.json. Examples: \"rebind ctrl+s\", \"add a chord shortcut\", \"change the submit key\", \"customize keybindings\".\n- verify: Verify that a code change actually does what it's supposed to by exercising it end-to-end and observing behavior — drive the affected flow, not just tests or typecheck. Run before committing nontrivial changes; bootstraps this repo's project verify skill if none exists yet. Don't invoke it on a diff that only touches tests, docs, or other code with no runtime surface to drive (a change to product source always has one) — there's nothing to observe.\n- code-review: Review the current diff for correctness bugs and reuse/simplification/efficiency cleanups at the given effort level (low/medium: fewer, high-confidence findings; high→max: broader coverage, may include uncertain findings; ultra: deep multi-agent review in the cloud). Pass --comment to post findings as inline PR comments, or --fix to apply the findings to the working tree after the review.\n- simplify: Review the changed code for reuse, simplification, efficiency, and altitude cleanups, then apply the fixes. Quality only — it does not hunt for bugs; use /code-review for that.\n- fewer-permission-prompts: Scan your transcripts for common read-only Bash and MCP tool calls, then add a prioritized allowlist to project .claude/settings.json to reduce permission prompts.\n- loop: Run a prompt or slash command on a recurring interval (e.g. /loop 5m /foo). Omit the interval to let