{"parentUuid":"9e94792c-e924-407e-98ef-e1f3d2840b18","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-fable-5","id":"msg_011CcpohdseRy4bctsv4drQg","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01C6Hup7kYBaqmbf4L9GLV75","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/zenray/.claude/skills/think-of-everything/SKILL.md","content":"---\nname: think-of-everything\ndescription: Run a systematic impact and premortem pass before or after a code change, feature, or architecture decision. Reads the request four ways (literal, inverse, adjacent, reframed), greps the actual codebase for real dependencies instead of guessing from memory, checks a 12-axis impact surface (data/state, concurrency, quotas, security, errors, backward compatibility, performance, cost, observability, UX edges, tests, rollback), runs a narrative premortem, then delivers at most 5 ranked risks, each with file:line proof and a concrete verification step, ending in a GO / NO-GO verdict. Use this whenever the user says \"pense à tout\", \"think of everything\", \"qu'est-ce que j'ai oublié\", \"what am I missing\", asks what a change might break, or wants a premortem, dependency check, or impact analysis. Also trigger proactively, even without the exact phrase, right before shipping anything that touches shared state, storage or quotas, concurrency, auth or permissions, payments, or a public API/route.\n---\n\n# Think of Everything (Pense à Tout)\n\nOne goal: surface the FEW risks that actually matter, each proven against the real code, each with a concrete check, ending in a decision. 40 generic checklist items is a failure; 5 proven, ranked risks is a success. Normal Q&A answers the question asked. This pass answers it, its opposite, and the ones nobody asked, then verifies against the codebase instead of memory.\n\n## Step 0: Classify and scale (one line)\n\n- Type: (a) change to make, (b) existing code or diff to audit, (c) concept with no code yet.\n- Mode (b): the source of truth is `git diff` and the actual files, never the description of the change.\n- Trivial change (copy, comment, isolated constant): say so, jump straight to Step 4 + verdict, 5 output lines max. The full pass is reserved for shared state, storage/quotas, concurrency, auth, money, public APIs.\n\n## Step 1: Read the request four ways (one line each)\n\n1. **Literal**: restate exactly what was asked.\n2. **Inverse**: what if the premise is false? What single input or condition breaks this? And what does NOT doing it cost (the baseline that makes the risk worth taking or not)?\n3. **Adjacent**: the 2-3 questions a senior engineer would ask next, unprompted. Parallel questions, not follow-ups.\n4. **Reframed**: the clearest version of this request. If it differs from the literal one, answer that too.\n\n## Step 2: Trace real dependencies (grep, never memory)\n\n**Evidence rule, the core of this skill: every claim \"X depends on Y\" cites a file:line found by searching IN THIS SESSION, or carries the label [non vérifié]. No exceptions.**\n\nSearch in this order:\n\n- **Callers and consumers**: `rg -n \"<symbol|route|table|key>\"` across the whole repo. Every call site, not just the edited file.\n- **Hyrum's law**: who depends on the CURRENT undocumented behavior — output format, ordering, timing, error text, casing? Those are the breaks nobody sees coming.\n- **Shared state**: files, DB, cache, env, config, globals that other code also touches.\n- **Data already written**: does the new code re-read data produced by the old code? Implicit migration is a top real-world breaker.\n- **External contracts**: public API/URL/schema/file format, cron jobs, CI, deploy scripts, docs that describe the old behavior.\n\nNo repo access, or the code doesn't exist yet: label the whole map \"non vérifié / spéculatif\". Never present a guess as a finding.\n\n## Step 3: Impact surface (12 axes, one killer question each)\n\n1. **Data & state**: does existing data stay valid? Is a partial write possible?\n2. **Concurrency**: two at once on the same resource — then what?\n3. **Limits & quotas**: which cap (size, rate, count, disk) gets hit first, and what happens at the boundary?\n4. **Security & access**: who can trigger this, and against someone else's resource?\n5. **Errors & partial failure**: fails halfway — what state remains, is it recoverable?\n6. **Backward compatibility**: old data, old clients, old links still work?\n7. **Performance**: still fine at 100x current volume?\n8. **Cost**: recurring cost that scales with usage?\n9. **Observability**: if it breaks silently, how do you find out?\n10. **UX edges**: empty state, oversized input, duplicate action, offline?\n11. **Tests**: is there a test that fails if this specific behavior regresses?\n12. **Rollback**: one command to turn it off? Does data written in the meantime survive?\n\nKeep only the applicable axes. Dismiss the rest in one line (\"N/A: ...\"), never pad.\n\n## Step 4: Premortem (narrative, not checklist)\n\n\"Three weeks in production. It broke, and it was THIS change. Write the most plausible incident, then the second.\" Check the high-base-rate classics first: empty/first-run state, null or empty list, off-by-one, encoding/timezone, non-idempotent retry, stale cache, two concurrent writers, quota hit.\n\n## Step 5: Rank, cut, decide\n\n- Rank each risk: severity (cosmetic → data loss/irreversible) x likelihood. Irreversible bumps severity one level.\n- Keep the top 5 maximum. Dismissed risks get one summary line total.\n- Every kept risk carries its **proof** (file:line or [non vérifié]) and **one concrete check** to confirm or kill it (a command, a test case, a grep).\n\n## Output format (compact, French by default on this account)\n\n```\nChangement : 1 à 3 lignes.\nAngles morts (4 lectures) : 1 ligne chacune.\n| # | Risque | Preuve | Sév x Prob | Vérification |\nÉcartés : 1 ligne.\nQuestions ouvertes : numérotées, chacune avec un défaut recommandé (accord par silence).\nVerdict : GO / GO avec corrections (listées) / NO-GO + correction minimale.\n```\n\n## Stop conditions and anti-goals\n\n- **ONE pass.** Re-run only if the diff changed afterwards, and only on the delta. Never apply this skill to its own output.\n- Code, architecture, and product decisions only. Not personal or business life-planning (the standing 48h-7day advice cap applies).\n- Not a substitute for tests or CI: this catches known failure classes, not novel ones. Say that plainly instead of implying completeness.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8187,"cache_read_input_tokens":79234,"output_tokens":7686,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":8187,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":7686,"cache_read_input_tokens":79234,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8187,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":8187},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CcpohctcZbJx5tcAGbYQ6","type":"assistant","uuid":"7a441393-afb8-4e80-bc77-a8d279f88c99","timestamp":"2026-07-08T15:14:00.196Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/Create/Build/Memory","sessionId":"12274ad5-32d7-47cc-a6d0-0a86ac33e4ed","version":"2.1.202","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"7a441393-afb8-4e80-bc77-a8d279f88c99","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"6a49cd17-0053-456a-8bbd-04050b8fc169","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01C6Hup7kYBaqmbf4L9GLV75","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /Users/zenray/.claude/skills/think-of-everything/SKILL.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"daa0f977-f203-4d9b-a560-c89b923b6ea4","timestamp":"2026-07-08T15:14:07.341Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"update","filePath":"/Users/zenray/.claude/skills/think-of-everything/SKILL.md","content":"---\nname: think-of-everything\ndescription: Run a systematic impact and premortem pass before or after a code change, feature, or architecture decision. Reads the request four ways (literal, inverse, adjacent, reframed), greps the actual codebase for real dependencies instead of guessing from memory, checks a 12-axis impact surface (data/state, concurrency, quotas, security, errors, backward compatibility, performance, cost, observability, UX edges, tests, rollback), runs a narrative premortem, then delivers at most 5 ranked risks, each with file:line proof and a concrete verification step, ending in a GO / NO-GO verdict. Use this whenever the user says \"pense à tout\", \"think of everything\", \"qu'est-ce que j'ai oublié\", \"what am I missing\", asks what a change might break, or wants a premortem, dependency check, or impact analysis. Also trigger proactively, even without the exact phrase, right before shipping anything that touches shared state, storage or quotas, concurrency, auth or permissions, payments, or a public API/route.\n---\n\n# Think of Everything (Pense à Tout)\n\nOne goal: surface the FEW risks that actually matter, each proven against the real code, each with a concrete check, ending in a decision. 40 generic checklist items is a failure; 5 proven, ranked risks is a success. Normal Q&A answers the question asked. This pass answers it, its opposite, and the ones nobody asked, then verifies against the codebase instead of memory.\n\n## Step 0: Classify and scale (one line)\n\n- Type: (a) change to make, (b) existing code or diff to audit, (c) concept with no code yet.\n- Mode (b): the source of truth is `git diff` and the actual files, never the description of the change.\n- Trivial change (copy, comment, isolated constant): say so, jump straight to Step 4 + verdict, 5 output lines max. The full pass is reserved for shared state, storage/quotas, concurrency, auth, money, public APIs.\n\n## Step 1: Read the request four ways (one line each)\n\n1. **Literal**: restate exactly what was asked.\n2. **Inverse**: what if the premise is false? What single input or condition breaks this? And what does NOT doing it cost (the baseline that makes the risk worth taking or not)?\n3. **Adjacent**: the 2-3 questions a senior engineer would ask next, unprompted. Parallel questions, not follow-ups.\n4. **Reframed**: the clearest version of this request. If it differs from the literal one, answer that too.\n\n## Step 2: Trace real dependencies (grep, never memory)\n\n**Evidence rule, the core of this skill: every claim \"X depends on Y\" cites a file:line found by searching IN THIS SESSION, or carries the label [non vérifié]. No exceptions.**\n\nSearch in this order:\n\n- **Callers and consumers**: `rg -n \"<symbol|route|table|key>\"` across the whole repo. Every call site, not just the edited file.\n- **Hyrum's law**: who depends on the CURRENT undocumented behavior — output format, ordering, timing, error text, casing? Those are the breaks nobody sees coming.\n- **Shared state**: files, DB, cache, env, config, globals that other code also touches.\n- **Data already written**: does the new code re-read data produced by the old code? Implicit migration is a top real-world breaker.\n- **External contracts**: public API/URL/schema/file format, cron jobs, CI, deploy scripts, docs that describe the old behavior.\n\nNo repo access, or the code doesn't exist yet: label the whole map \"non vérifié / spéculatif\". Never present a guess as a finding.\n\n## Step 3: Impact surface (12 axes, one killer question each)\n\n1. **Data & state**: does existing data stay valid? Is a partial write possible?\n2. **Concurrency**: two at once on the same resource — then what?\n3. **Limits & quotas**: which cap (size, rate, count, disk) gets hit first, and what happens at the boundary?\n4. **Security & access**: who can trigger this, and against someone else's resource?\n5. **Errors & partial failure**: fails halfway — what state remains, is it recoverable?\n6. **Backward compatibility**: old data, old clients, old links still work?\n7. **Performance**: still fine at 100x current volume?\n8. **Cost**: recurring cost that scales with usage?\n9. **Observability**: if it breaks silently, how do you find out?\n10. **UX edges**: empty state, oversized input, duplicate action, offline?\n11. **Tests**: is there a test that fails if this specific behavior regresses?\n12. **Rollback**: one command to turn it off? Does data written in the meantime survive?\n\nKeep only the applicable axes. Dismiss the rest in one line (\"N/A: ...\"), never pad.\n\n## Step 4: Premortem (narrative, not checklist)\n\n\"Three weeks in production. It broke, and it was THIS change. Write the most plausible incident, then the second.\" Check the high-base-rate classics first: empty/first-run state, null or empty list, off-by-one, encoding/timezone, non-idempotent retry, stale cache, two concurrent writers, quota hit.\n\n## Step 5: Rank, cut, decide\n\n- Rank each risk: severity (cosmetic → data loss/irreversible) x likelihood. Irreversible bumps severity one level.\n- Keep the top 5 maximum. Dismissed risks get one summary line total.\n- Every kept risk carries its **proof** (file:line or [non vérifié]) and **one concrete check** to confirm or kill it (a command, a test case, a grep).\n\n## Output format (compact, French by default on this account)\n\n```\nChangement : 1 à 3 lignes.\nAngles morts (4 lectures) : 1 ligne chacune.\n| # | Risque | Preuve | Sév x Prob | Vérification |\nÉcartés : 1 ligne.\nQuestions ouvertes : numérotées, chacune avec un défaut recommandé (accord par silence).\nVerdict : GO / GO avec corrections (listées) / NO-GO + correction minimale.\n```\n\n## Stop conditions and anti-goals\n\n- **ONE pass.** Re-run only if the diff changed afterwards, and only on the delta. Never apply this skill to its own output.\n- Code, architecture, and product decisions only. Not personal or business life-planning (the standing 48h-7day advice cap applies).\n- Not a substitute for tests or CI: this catches known failure classes, not novel ones. Say that plainly instead of implying completeness.\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":86,"newStart":1,"newLines":79,"lines":[" ---"," name: think-of-everything","-description: Run a systematic \"think of everything\" impact and premortem pass before or after implementing a code change, feature, or architecture decision. Reads the request four ways (literal, inverse, adjacent, reframed), traces every real interdependency in the actual codebase by grepping or searching it directly instead of guessing from memory, then checks the change against a fixed impact-surface list covering data and state, concurrency, quotas, security, error handling, backward compatibility, performance, cost, observability, UX edge cases, tests, and rollback. Use this whenever the user says \"pense à tout\", \"think of everything\", \"qu'est-ce que j'ai oublié\", \"what am I missing\", asks what a change might break, or wants a premortem, dependency check, or impact analysis. Also trigger proactively, even without the exact phrase, right before shipping anything that touches shared state, storage or quotas, concurrency, auth or permissions, payments, or a public API/route.","+description: Run a systematic impact and premortem pass before or after a code change, featu